<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6486"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:blingbling1169@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Cawichitan<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cawichitan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 07, 2005 - 4:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I have said from the beginning of this whole brick thing that further analysis of the brick can bring more information than that box of cereal-not that I don&#39;t think it can possibly be linked to the killer, I just think he is a little smarter than leaving anything pertainent in a box of cereal. I think he saw the box and thought to himself &#34;well, I&#39;ll just use this-if he left it at all. He wants to be caught. WPD is just holding back his info. He wants us to be able to hear all the clues. Why enlist our help if they are just gonna keep everything to themselves anyway? Why waste John Walsh&#39; time if they aren&#39;t going to release the latest information? Why won&#39;t they just admit that they need our help? They still haven&#39;t found the man in Wichita that murdered my boyfriend in 97 and they say he was nothing more than a gang member-if they can&#39;t find him, how can they find BTK? We need the clues! BTK IS SMART, THEY BETTER LET US HELP THEM OUT! I think the box was a vessle, not the big clue. I think that the brick will say more. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6486-->
<!--Post: 6494--><!--Time: 1107826843-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6494"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Wade<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wade<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 07, 2005 - 5:40 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It&#39;s just a damn brick.  It doesn&#39;t talk or dance or anything special.  It&#39;s a damn brick!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6494-->
<!--Post: 6509--><!--Time: 1107840632-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6509"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 07, 2005 - 9:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Wade, you&#39;re probably right however the yellow brick road led to the Emerald City so maybe the brick on the side of the road leads to the pearl necklace.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6509-->
<!--Post: 6543--><!--Time: 1107878939-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6543"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Whoisbtk<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Whoisbtk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 8:08 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Audreyamw <BR> <BR>Thanks for the link. <BR> <BR>This brick is interesting for lots of reasons: <BR> <BR>1.	The area where the box was left is rural.  There is nothing nearby to get a brick from; there are no other loose bricks in the area.  BTK brought the brick with him. <BR>2.	The wooden post the box is leaning against is 4x4.  The brick is longer than the width of the post, and appears to be 6 inches long.  The height and depth look roughly equal, and are roughly 2/3 of the length.  The rough dimensions are: 6x4x4. <BR>3.	Number the sides of the brick this way: <BR>a.	Top is #1 <BR>b.	Bottom is #6 <BR>c.	Front long face is #2, then clockwise around the sides <BR>d.	Left short side is #3, <BR>e.	Back long side is #4, and  <BR>f.	Right short side is #5  <BR> <BR>Looking at this photo &#40;<a href="http://ghostiesannex.myphotoalbum.com/view_photo.php?set_albumName=BTK&amp;id=cereal90ln" target=_top>http://ghostiesannex.myphotoalbum.com/view_photo.p hp?set_albumName=BTK&amp;id=cereal90ln</a>} <BR>2 and 3 are visible along with part of 1.  2 and 3 are clean.  The weathering and staining seen on 1 are not seen on 2 or 3.   These sides &#40;2,3&#41; were protected from weather and wear while 1 was not.  There is no visible trace of mortar on 2 or 3, and there are no marks where mortar was chipped/scraped away. <BR> <BR>The brick is clearly not new.  1 is much darker than 2 or 3.  It has a bluish tint that is characteristic of ageing.  The left side edge of 1 is broken; the surface exposed by the break has aged the same way as the rest of 1, indicating that the break is old, and that the brick stayed in place after the damage.  This tends to support the notion that the brick was not used for cosmetic purposes. <BR> <BR>The brick is old.  The color and condition of 1 show age and its too thick for modern patio or sidewalk pavers. <BR> <BR>The marked difference in wear and damage between 1 and 2 or 3 indicates that the brick was set in a wall or paved surface with 1 as the exposed face; 2 and 3 are protected by whatever matrix was used to set the bricks. <BR> <BR>The brick was probably not part of a wall.  Mortar or its equivalent is used in a wall.  It has to adhere to the hidden surfaces of the bricks or gravity pulls the wall apart.  The lack of mortar adhering to the hidden/protected surfaces &#40;2 and 3&#41; probably rules out a wall. <BR> <BR>Surface paving is a different story.  Bricks can be dry-set on a bed of tamped gravel and sand brushed into the joints.  This would account for the clean appearance of 2 and 3. <BR> <BR>So, where does one get a brick like this?  From a Kansas streets paved from 1920 to 1940. <BR> <BR>Brick streets were made of a concrete base covered with sand and then bricks.  Some have lasted 70 years without significant repair.  James Garfield Brown who paved 4161/2 feet of road with 46,664 bricks in one day claimed the record for one man laying paving bricks September 12 1925 in Olathe Kansas. <a href="http://www.goodlandnet.com/history/brickstreets.htm" target=_top>http://www.goodlandnet.com/history/brickstreets.ht m</a> <BR> <BR> Most Kansas cities still have brick streets that were paved in the 20s and 30s with 6x4x4 paving bricks.  The intervening years have weathered them to a bluish purple brown and traffic has worn the surfaces.  Bricks are a low grade ceramic with wide variations in hardness.  Hard bricks stay rough, soft ones wear smooth.  This looks like one of the hard ones. <BR> <BR>Kansas brick makers, as advertising, commonly cast their name on the top of the brick along with the year it was made.  The name on the brick would tell who made it, and probably the town it came from.   <BR> <BR>Bricks like this are not hard to find.  As the streets were repaired, sewer lines dug etc. bricks were replaced with concrete or asphalt.  The old bricks were discarded or recycled into walls, garden borders in nearby homes, or similar non-government use.   <BR> <BR>BTK brought the brick with him; it’s not just something he picked up after he got there.  It was a conscious choice.  He could have taped it to the pole, but he chose to bring this brick. <BR> <BR>Two questions: why this brick and where did he get it? <BR> <BR>I’m stumped on “why”, but “where” has an answer. <BR> <BR>Unless BTK pulled it fresh from a street, the lack of dirt on the sides indicates storage in a protected environment.   <BR> <BR>Who stores old paving bricks indoors? <BR> <BR>Old bricks have value as collectables <a href="http://www.msinter.net/tweety/" target=_top>http://www.msinter.net/tweety/</a>. It’s not unusual for small town antique stores sell them.   <BR> <BR>Did I mention that Kechi bills itself as the “Antique Capital of Kansas”? <a href="http://www.kechiks.com/antiques.php" target=_top>http://www.kechiks.com/antiques.php</a>. <BR> <BR>I think I’ll do some shopping. <BR> <BR><a href="mailto:whoisbtk@yahoo.com">whoisbtk@yahoo.com</a> <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6543-->
<!--Post: 6544--><!--Time: 1107879699-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6544"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Wade<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wade<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 8:21 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->OMG....its a freaking brick.  So what if it&#39;s clean on one side.  I got all kinds stacked up in back.  The tops are clean on all of them and the sides are clean on all the ones not on the exterior.  I am a landscaper, trust me, there is nothing special about that particular brick. <BR>So what if he brought the brick with him.  What does that mean???? <BR> <BR>Johno-  The emerald city was make believe.....geeeeeshh....btk is laughing his ass off now. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6544-->
<!--Post: 6557--><!--Time: 1107887352-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6557"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Whoisbtk<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Whoisbtk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 10:29 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hey. <BR> <BR>Thanks for clearing that up for me.  BTK probably drives a truck with a pile of bricks in the back. <BR> <BR>Or maybe he carries a brick around to put on his cereal box in case the wind tries to blow it off his picnic blanket. <BR> <BR>Don&#39;t know what I was thinking. <BR> <BR>Thanks again. <BR> <BR><a href="mailto:whoisbtk@yahoo.com">whoisbtk@yahoo.com</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6557-->
<!--Post: 6558--><!--Time: 1107888289-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6558"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Nancydrew<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nancydrew<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 10:44 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Whoisbtk:  I sincerely mean this with the utmost respect...I think it&#39;s time to step back and put this in perspective.  It&#39;s a brick.  I have tons of respect for you and have enjoyed your posts ever since I found these boards.  Please don&#39;t take offense, but it might be time to step out for a little fresh air and enjoy a non-BTK related hobby for an hour or two.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6558-->
<!--Post: 6559--><!--Time: 1107889075-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6559"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 10:57 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I have to agree with Nancy, but also say that Whoisbtk shouldn&#39;t feel singled out - this kind of thing happens to every single one of us who look too long and too hard at this in some manner. I&#39;ve caught myself writing out a very long and involved post about one thing or another only to realize what I&#39;m saying is a whole lotta nothing about something. It&#39;s really easy to do after a while, because there&#39;s actually not that much for the public to go on, and what is there is sometimes dubious. &#40;For instance, how many of us doubt the veracity of a majority of the things released on 11/30/04? BTK would never be above leading us down the primrose path just for his own amusement.&#41; <BR> <BR>Nancy&#39;s suggestion is a good one for all of us from time to time, not just whoisbtk.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6559-->
<!--Post: 6560--><!--Time: 1107889326-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6560"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:hackenmaster@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Wichitan<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wichitan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 11:02 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->whoisbtk, thank you for your thoughts, I can see that you put alot of time in your theory, and appreciate it.  This is a theory thread, and your comment belongs here.  I am not closed minded to hearing your thoughts and ideas, and you stayed on topic....great job.  On  a side note the &#34;brick&#34; was found in Park City, I have been to Kechi Antiques Mall, and have never seen a brick for sale.  You think it is &#34;possible&#34; that during the Davis Killing, the brick was one of the things BTK might have taken with him?  Maybe this would explain the weathered/non-weathered look.  Or maybe that big chunk of the top brock and is what was left for police to determine that a &#34;brick&#34; had been used to break the back door.  I ask everyone to be somewhat open minded. <BR> <BR>wichitan<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6560-->
<!--Post: 6563--><!--Time: 1107891026-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6563"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Nancydrew<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nancydrew<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 11:30 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I hear you, Steve.  I&#39;ve done the same thing.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6563-->
<!--Post: 6575--><!--Time: 1107898285-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6575"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Whoisbtk<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Whoisbtk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 1:31 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;O wad some Power the giftie gie us <BR>To see oursels as inthers see us! <BR>It wad frae monie a blunder free us, <BR>An&#39; foolish notion;&#34; <BR> <BR>Robert Burns: Ode to a Louse <BR> <BR>OK nancydrew <BR> <BR>good suggestion <BR> <BR>thanks <BR> <BR><a href="mailto:whoisbtk@yahoo.com">whoisbtk@yahoo.com</a> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6575-->
<!--Post: 6594--><!--Time: 1107903435-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6594"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lorenap<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lorenap<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 2:57 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->or.... When they finally catch this guy and they ask him about the brick he may surprise us. IT MAY MEAN SOMETHING.... ahhh then we&#39;ll all have egg on our face&#39;s because we doubted the significance of it.  <BR> <BR>I say after you take your walk outside &#40;whois&#41; get back to your sleuthing. LE needs a few more like you.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6594-->
<!--Post: 6597--><!--Time: 1107903870-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6597"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 3:04 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Aren&#39;t there any rocks in Kansas?  <BR>Why not use a rock to weigh down the box?  <BR>Since BTK is proficient at binding and knots, so much so that he uses the moniker Bind as his first name, then why not tie the box to the post? <BR>It has been said by a witness who lives in the area where the Post Toastie box was found that there were two toasties boxes at different locations in the area. Both identical and held down by a brick. One box of which we have photos had been recently put where it was found and had a brick and red crepe streamers on top of it. The other box was starting to fade from exposure to the elements so it had been there longer but it also had a brick on top of it and was festooned with yellow crepe streamers. <BR>So BTK had two bricks and I think determining the origin of where the bricks came from are equally as important to what store the boxes were purchased in. Even if the brick has no meaning and was simply as some believe a weight the brick can still reveal a lot of information. <BR>Remember too that there is a construction theme running through some BTK victims. Nancy Fox worked for a construction company and so did Shirley Vian&#39;s common-law husband while Vicki Wegerle&#39;s husband was a self-employed matainence man. <BR>A construction worker can get away with wearing gloves even in the summer. The post office employee in 1979 said the guy who gave her the package for KAKE was dressed in blue jeans, blue jean jacket and wearing gloves. Construction guy? Maybe. Bricks from a demolition construction site? Maybe. <BR>Let&#39;s say the bricks are a mere paperwieght. OK, but lets also find their origin and possible link to an occupation. <BR>Let&#39;s say they are part of a clue. OK, how and why?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6597-->
<!--Post: 6603--><!--Time: 1107906206-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6603"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 3:43 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Johno, the connection to construction/trades for the BTK victims could be just a coincidence.  His victims were blue-collar, from what I gather, so it would figure that a lot of them would be married to someone who worked in construction, or in the trades. <BR> <BR>Personally, I think the brick was to make the WPD and maybe the public believe that he&#39;s connected to the murder of Dee Davis, the victim who had a brick thrown through the glass in her back door.   <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t think BTK works in construction, or any blue collar job, at least not since he left WSU.  So many SK seem to go after victims that they see as weak, inferior, and &#39;beneath&#39; them somehow.  Gary Ridgeway thought that prostitutes were rubbish and he targeted them. Wayne Williams &#40;Atlanta child killings&#41; went after street kids.   Dahmer killed minorities.  I see BTK as being in this vein, since he went after blue collar victims, and is literate, computer savvy and probably university educated.  The fireman who saw BTK make his Nancy Fox call could only remember that BTK may have been wearing a work uniform of some sort, like a phone company repairman or a similar kind.  BTK may have gotten a kick out of dressing up in his little blue-collar uniform when he went off to kill his working class victims. The uniform may have been part of his fantasy -- he may have imagined himself as a military-style executioner at the crime scenes. <BR> <BR>What does this have to do with the brick?  I&#39;m running off on a tangent.  The brick indicates that BTK probably lives in town.  He took it with him out to the rural road.  BTK is a townie.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6603-->
<!--Post: 6612--><!--Time: 1107908473-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6612"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 4:21 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Audreyamw, well now you are getting somewhere with your brick analysis. You have reinforced a probable earlier feeling that you had that BTK is from urban Wichita. <BR>This is why I feel this brick is more important than it seems on the surface. <BR>You may be correct with your belief that BTK doen&#39;t work in construction but either way lets examine this brick a little further before we dismiss it as a dead end. <BR>I can see the headline: BTK Tripped Up By A Brick.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6612-->
<!--Post: 6659--><!--Time: 1107928980-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6659"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Kansasgirl<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Kansasgirl<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 10:03 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Where has it been noted that there were two Post Toasties Boxes. I haven&#39;t heard this before. Is this just heresay?? <BR> <BR>Here in Kansas there are numerous towns with numerous brick streets &#40;too many to count&#41;and most of them have gaping holes with missing bricks. Due to the time when they were placed, I highly doubt more than one or two companies provided the bricks as well as placed the bricks. All are going to be very similar. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6659-->
<!--Post: 6666--><!--Time: 1107930857-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6666"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:hackenmaster@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Wichitan<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wichitan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 10:34 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->my problem is BTK put the brick on the top, not inside at the bottom of the box.  If I wanted to weight something like that down, brick first, contents, and the the final tape job. <BR> <BR>wichitan<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6666-->
<!--Post: 6669--><!--Time: 1107935233-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6669"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 08, 2005 - 11:47 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Kansasgirl, a 31 year old nurse Jennifer Gonzales who drives the route where the red ribboned Post toasties box was found said that she first noticed that box on January 20th several days before it was photographed by the televison station and recovered by police. Gonzales also said that on Saturday, January 22nd she saw a second box with yellow streamers under a No Dumping sign south of 101st St. North. That box was later not there and it is unknown if the police or someone else took it if it was there at all.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6669-->
